  {
    "episode_id": 49919,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49919.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 76,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think that it's fair to say that America is in an irritable mood. I don't think there's much question about that, but not to miss the point. that I think elections are about investing in our institutions and not investing in our personalities. So I still feel very good about America's institutions. I think that we've been through periods before, which were far more, I think, in upheaval than we are currently. And after all, we fought a civil war in the 1860s. We've had two world wars. We've had all kinds of dissent that our First Amendment acknowledges the obligation of a second opinion. So I think that democracy is supposed to be noisy, and I don't think that I'm trying to understate the polarization that has now settled in with the American people, but I also feel very confident about our institutions.",
